        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> First off: I love the `mini` plugin library. It has replaced lots of other separate plugins for me and made Neovim loads and loads faster. Thank you for all your hard work!\r\n\r\n:heart:\r\n\r\n\r\n> Does this mean you are not planning to ever implement it? Or can you still be persuaded to do so? :) I can very much appreciate the minimalist approach for `mini.tabline`. The only functionality I currently miss is exactly the one above: custom positioning/ordering for buffers in the tabline.\r\n> \r\n> It's a functionality I used extremely much when I still used `BarBar`, as it allows a user to visually pair tabs together that are related in any form or manner. For example, one of my use cases was to pair the tab of an open implementation file together with its test file. This allowed for fast and easy buffer switching.\r\n> \r\n> I searched through the closed issues for the tabline module and did not come across this request before, hence my asking regardless of your comment in the documentation. Apologies if this request is unwanted.\r\n\r\nCustom order was indeed not even planned to be supported. Mostly because it was not easy to implement for me at the time, but also because it would mean extra functions (like `next_tab()`/`prev_tab()`) instead of already present `:bnext`/`:bprev`.\r\n\r\nThat said I do also wish for custom order possibility for the exact reason you described. It is far from urgent, though, as there is `:b#` command which takes you to alternate file (the buffer you previously visited) which can be mapped to a more easier mapping (I use `<Leader>ba`; some like `<BS>`).\r\n\r\nI'll think about it, but at the moment it is definitely not the top priority.",
