        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Thanks for your fast response! Yes, I can imagine it's much more work. You would have to to keep track of the buffer positions by yourself instead of relying on neovim's numbering/indexing.\r\n\r\n> [...] but also because it would mean extra functions (like next_tab()/prev_tab()) instead of already present :bnext/:bprev.\r\n\r\nI suppose the new functions would become something like `move_buf_prev()` and `move_buf_next()`, and `:bprev/:bnext` would just keep on existing and functioning like they do :)\r\n\r\n> It is far from urgent, though, as there is :b# command which takes you to alternate file (the buffer you previously visited) which can be mapped to a more easier mapping (I use <Leader>ba; some like <BS>).\r\n\r\nYeah, although that is more of an alternative to navigating the buffers. It's not an alternative to moving the buffer tabs to a different location on the tabline, unfortunately.\r\n\r\n> I'll think about it, but at the moment it is definitely not the top priority.\r\n\r\nI completely understand it's not high on the list :) Thanks a lot for taking it into consideration at all!",
